Machine Learning (ML) is a subset of AI where systems learn patterns from data instead of being explicitly programmed with fixed rules — the model improves its predictions by being trained on 
examples, rather than someone writing if-else logic for every case.

Where it fits in what we discussed earlier:

AI
 └── Machine Learning ← here
       └── Deep Learning
             └── LLM (Llama3, Claude, ChatGPT)

training an LLM is also machine learning?

Yes — exactly right. Training an LLM is machine learning, specifically a more advanced/deep branch of it.

AI
 └── Machine Learning
       └── Deep Learning (uses neural networks with many layers)
             └── LLM training (deep learning applied specifically to language)
                   └── Llama3 / Claude / ChatGPT (the resulting trained models)

Deep Learning
 ├── CNNs (Convolutional Neural Networks) → Face unlock, image recognition
 ├── RNNs/Transformers for audio → Voice assistants (speech-to-text)
 ├── Neural collaborative filtering → Recommendation engines
 └── LLMs (transformer architecture, trained on TEXT specifically) → Llama3, Claude, ChatGPT

To be precise about the "Deep Learning" layer in between:
LLM training specifically uses deep learning — neural networks with many stacked layers (in this case, a "transformer" architecture) — which is why it sits one level deeper than general ML in the 
hierarchy. Not all machine learning is deep learning (e.g., a simple fraud-detection decision tree is ML but NOT deep learning), but all LLM training is both ML and deep learning.

Machine Learning (broad)
 ├── Classic ML (decision trees, regression, clustering — simpler, no neural networks)
 └── Deep Learning (neural networks with many layers)
       └── LLMs (Llama3, Claude, ChatGPT)

Classic ML (NOT deep learning) — simpler algorithms, no neural networks:

| Example                                      | Technique                                  |
|----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| Decision trees (loan approval)               | Decision tree algorithm                    |
| Linear regression (house price prediction)   | Statistical regression                     |
| Customer segmentation/clustering             | Clustering algorithms (k-means, etc.)      |
| Simple/older spam filters                    | Naive Bayes, logistic regression           |


Deep Learning (a SUBSET of ML — uses neural networks with many layers):

| Example                                      | Why it's deep learning                                                |
|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Face unlock                                  | Uses convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to recognize facial patterns — genuinely deep learning                 |
| Voice assistants (Siri/Alexa)                | Speech-to-text uses deep neural networks (often transformer-based now)                                           |
| Modern Netflix/YouTube recommendations       | Often uses deep neural networks now, though simpler versions use classic ML too — depends on the company's system|
| LLMs (Llama3, Claude, ChatGPT)               | Deep learning specifically (transformer architecture)                                                            |


Why they're different, specifically:

| System                    | What it processes                              | Is it an LLM?                                                                                                                                                                           |
|---------------------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Face unlock               | Images (pixels)                                | No — uses CNNs, built for image recognition, not language                                                                                                                               |
| Voice assistants          | Audio waveforms → converts to text             | No — uses speech-recognition-specific neural networks (though the response generation part of Siri/Alexa might use an LLM-like component today, the voice recognition itself isn't one) |
| Recommendation engines    | User behavior data (clicks, watch history)     | No — uses recommendation-specific neural networks, not language models                                                                                                                  |
| Llama3/Claude/ChatGPT     | Text                                           | Yes — specifically designed and trained to understand/generate language                                                                                                                 |

