Types of ML

Machine Learning has 5 main types, categorized by how the model learns from data:

1. Supervised Learning — learns from labeled data (input + correct answer given)

| Example                | Input                 | Label                      |
|------------------------|-----------------------|----------------------------|
| Fraud detection        | Transaction details   | "Fraud" or "Not fraud"     |
| Spam filter            | Email content         | "Spam" or "Not spam"       |
| House price prediction | Size, location        | Actual sale price          |


2. Unsupervised Learning — learns patterns from unlabeled data (no "correct answer" given, just finds structure)

| Example                                      | What it does                                                   |
|---------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
| Customer segmentation                        | Groups customers by behavior, without being told the groups    |
| Anomaly detection (Grafana/Prometheus-style) | Learns "normal" patterns, flags anything unusual               |
| Topic modeling                               | Groups similar documents/articles together automatically       |


3. Reinforcement Learning — learns by trial and error, using rewards/penalties (no labeled data at all)

| Example                                      | What it does                                                         |
|---------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| AlphaGo (from our earlier discussion)        | Plays millions of games against itself, learns from win/loss rewards |
| Self-driving cars                            | Learns driving decisions through simulated trial and error           |
| Robotics                                     | Learns to walk/grasp objects through repeated attempts + rewards     |

4. Semi-supervised Learning — mix of labeled + unlabeled data (small amount labeled, large amount unlabeled)

• Common when labeling data is expensive/time-consuming — model learns from a small labeled set, then generalizes to the larger unlabeled set.

5. self-supervised learning

LLM training (like Llama3/Claude) is mostly a form of self-supervised learning — a variant where the data "labels itself." For text, the model predicts the next word in a sentence, using the 
actual next word as the "label" — no human manually labeled billions of sentences; the structure of language itself provides the training signal.

Simple summary table:

| Type                      | Needs labels?        | Example                                           |
|---------------------------|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| Supervised                | Yes                  | Fraud detection                                   |
| Unsupervised              | No                   | Customer segmentation                             |
| Reinforcement             | No (uses rewards)    | AlphaGo                                           |
| Semi-supervised           | Partial              | Medical image classification (few labeled scans)  |
| Self-supervised (LLMs)    | Data labels itself   | Llama3, Claude, ChatGPT                           |
